  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Saturday...\n\nCurrent analysis is showing a low pressure system moving through\nnorthern Washington with the associated frontal passage moving\nthrough western MT. This front is expected to make its way into\nsouth central MT during the early morning hours today, bringing \nscattered showers and isolated thunderstorms. With limited \ninstability during the morning, thunderstorms will likely be on \nthe weaker side. Most hi-res models do show additional showers and\nthunderstorms behind the initial frontal push, but there is some \nuncertainty on if the atmosphere can gain some instability back by\nmid day. That being said, there are only isolated pockets of CAPE\nvalues of 500-700 J/kg, generally for areas Billings east. This \nwill lend way to a few strong to severe thunderstorms mainly \nduring the afternoon. With much drier conditions expected behind \nthe front, strong wind gusts to 60 mph will be the main threat \nwith some small hail possible. Following the front, strong \nsubsidence will lead to breezy conditions across the region with \nwesterly wind gusts 25-40 mph late morning into the afternoon \nSunday, with gusts in the 20s (mph) as the low departs Monday. \nAdditionally, as conditions cool into the low 30s in the higher \nterrain, up to a inch of snow is possible in the highest peaks \n(generally above 10,000 ft) in the Beartooth/Absaroka and Crazy \nmountains. \n\nInto next week, brief ridging Monday will be followed by \nsouthwesterly flow aloft, leading to small daily chances of \nafternoon showers and thunderstorms Tuesday and Wednesday. There \nis still a lot of uncertainty regarding the track of the low over \nthe Pacific northwest flow which would affect precipitation \ncoverage and timing later in the week. \n\nHigh temperatures will be coolest today, in the 70s and low 80s.\nTemperatures will slightly warm back into the upper 70s and 80s,\nwith some places reaching the low 90s by late week. TS",
